The reward head contributes \textit{numerically negligible---zero to within floating-point precision} gradient to the temporal embedding. Exp\_016e's stop-gradient intervention detaches a gradient pathway that carries no signal---it is an architectural no-op. The null result is therefore expected: the intervention removes nothing.

The zero reward gradient is a consequence of the relative output dimensionality. All decoder losses backpropagate through the same RSSM latent state to reach $e_{\Delta t}$. The observation decoders reconstruct a 24-dimensional proprioceptive signal (14 orientations, 9 velocities, 1 height), while the reward head predicts a single scalar. This ${\sim}24\!\times$ dimensionality asymmetry, compounded by DreamerV3's symlog reward scaling which further compresses the reward gradient magnitude, renders the reward head's contribution numerically negligible---zero to within floating-point precision.


\subsubsection{Implications for the CIVO Mechanism}
\label{sec:sgs_implications}

The gradient decomposition reframes the mechanistic understanding of how the temporal embedding drives CIVO.

\paragraph{Observation reconstruction dominates temporal embedding learning.} The temporal embedding's parameters are shaped almost entirely (93\%) by the observation reconstruction objective (D061). This means $e_{\Delta t}$ learns to encode temporal information that helps minimize \textit{observation prediction error}, not reward prediction error. If this learned representation introduces systematic biases in latent state predictions at $\Delta t{>}1$---for instance, by encouraging the RSSM to produce states that are easy to decode but dynamically inaccurate---these biases propagate to \textit{all} downstream computation, including reward prediction and the critic's value estimates. CIVO thus originates in the observation reconstruction objective's influence on latent dynamics, not in reward-specific pathways.

\paragraph{Reframing the intervention target.} Any gradient-level strategy that targets the reward head alone---reward prediction regularization, reward-conditioned value clipping, or the reward-only SGS tested here---addresses $<$1\% of the gradient flow to $e_{\Delta t}$ and cannot attenuate CIVO. Effective gradient-level interventions must address the dominant observation decoder pathway. This motivates the \textit{full-pathway SGS} design (exp\_018): detaching $e_{\Delta t}$ from all decoder and reward gradients, retaining only the 7\% KL dynamics contribution. Full-pathway SGS removes 93\% of the gradient to $e_{\Delta t}$, constituting a genuine gradient-level intervention rather than the null operation of reward-only SGS.

\paragraph{Connection to RSSM fidelity analysis.} The observation-dominated gradient structure is consistent with the RSSM fidelity results in Table~\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity}: the trained-embedding condition (exp\_008d) shows a $2.78\times$ reward gap between $\Delta t{=}1$ and $\Delta t{=}2$ transitions, despite the reward head receiving zero gradient from $e_{\Delta t}$. This confirms that the reward head's $\Delta t$-dependent biases are inherited \textit{through the latent state}---which is shaped by observation reconstruction gradients---rather than learned directly from the reward objective.


\subsubsection{Exp\_016e Reinterpreted as Stochastic Variation}
\label{sec:016e_reinterp}

With the gradient decomposition establishing that exp\_016e is a no-op intervention, the apparent timing differences between exp\_016e and exp\_008d---later collapse onset (75K vs.\ 25K) and higher peak evaluation (326.7 vs.\ 167.2)---must be attributed to stochastic variation rather than SGS effects. This attribution is consistent with the seed-to-seed variability already observed within the trained-embedding condition: exp\_009b (same configuration as exp\_008d, seed 2) reaches peak evaluation 278.5 at 95K (exp\_009b) with transient NVE of $1.62\times$ at 45K before self-correcting, compared to exp\_008d's monotonic collapse from 25K. The qualitative diversity between exp\_008d (early collapse), exp\_009b (self-correction), and exp\_016e (delayed collapse) falls within the range of stochastic outcomes for a single trained-$e_{\Delta t}$ configuration.

All three trajectories share the invariant identified by the NVE diagnostic: each crosses the $1.5\times$ pathological threshold at some point during training (exp\_008d at 35K: $1.71\times$; exp\_009b at 45K: $1.62\times$; exp\_016e at 95K: $10.42\times$). Whether this pathological overestimation leads to sustained collapse or transient recovery depends on early training stochasticity, not on the reward-only SGS intervention (D061).

\subsection{Three Post-CIVO Outcomes: Why Seeds Diverge}
\label{sec:seed_divergence}

The three post-CIVO outcomes (Table~\ref{tab:post_civo})---full collapse, transient CIVO, and oscillatory degradation---all cross the $\nve > 1.5\times$ threshold, yet the downstream damage is stochastic.  We hypothesize that the divergence relates to the critic's representational capacity at the time of CIVO onset.  \citet{lyle2022capacity} demonstrate that deep RL networks suffer progressive capacity loss, in which early-learned features become increasingly difficult to overwrite.  If the critic's capacity is already partially consumed when CIVO injects overestimation, the network cannot recalibrate (seed~0); if sufficient capacity remains, the critic can absorb transient overestimation and recover (seed~2).  The oscillatory pattern (seed~1, 009e) represents an intermediate regime: the value function rises then reverses while evaluation oscillates, and entropy remains elevated---suggesting the actor has not fully committed to the overestimated policy but cannot escape it.  This partial capacity loss allows the critic to respond to new data without fully correcting its biases.

\subsection{Why MPC Is Immune}
\label{sec:mpc_immune}

\citet{nhu2025tawm} show that $\dt$-conditioning benefits TD-MPC2, raising the question of why it fails in DreamerV3.  The critical difference is that MPC re-plans from real states at every step---imagination errors do not accumulate into the training signal because there is no critic bootstrapping from imagined trajectories.  In DreamerV3, the actor-critic trains entirely within imagination, and any systematic reward-prediction bias at $\dt > 1$ is amplified through the closed actor-critic loop.  In terms of the two-stage decomposition (\S\ref{sec:civo_mechanism}), MPC is immune because Stage~2 cannot occur: without imagination-based bootstrapping, the actor cannot exploit the dynamics bias.  This aligns with \citet{frauenknecht2025rollouts}, who find that rollout quality dominates model-based RL performance, and with \citet{tallec2019making}'s analysis of how temporal resolution mismatches destabilize value learning.

\subsection{The \texorpdfstring{$\dt$}{dt}-Embedding Dilemma}
\label{sec:embedding_dilemma}

The factorial results reveal a fundamental tension: removing the $\dt$-embedding (008f) eliminates CIVO but at a $13\times$ cost to reward prediction quality ($0.7469$ vs.\ $0.0568$ MAE)---the world model cannot distinguish temporal scales.  Restricting imagination to $\dt{=}1$ while retaining the trained embedding (008e\_v3) prevents CIVO in 1 of 2 seeds but the other collapses via Pathway~B (\S\ref{sec:f6}).  Neither approach alone provides a complete solution.

\subsection{Multiple Vulnerability Pathways}
\label{sec:pathways}

The two collapse mechanisms identified in \S\ref{sec:f6}---CIVO (Pathway~A, value-driven, entropy decreasing) and non-CIVO instability (Pathway~B, eval-driven, entropy increasing)---have distinct diagnostic signatures but a shared root cause: the trained $\dt$-embedding's learned features affect both reward prediction across temporal scales (Pathway~A) and the representation space in which the actor operates (Pathway~B).  This explains why $\nve$ is sensitive but not perfectly specific: Pathway~B's late evaluation decline inflates $\nve$ through the same arithmetic as CIVO, yielding the sole false positive ($3.78\times$ for 008e\_v3 s2).  Distinguishing the two requires examining the temporal profile and entropy direction of collapse.  The existence of multiple pathways suggests that mitigation strategies focused exclusively on the imagination channel---restricting $\dt$, truncating rollouts, or smoothing rewards \citep{lee2024dreamsmooth}---may be insufficient.

\subsection{Implications for Temporal Abstraction in World Models}
\label{sec:implications}

CIVO identifies a structural incompatibility between flat temporal conditioning and imagination-based actor-critic training: the RSSM cannot maintain prediction fidelity across temporal resolutions when the embedding co-adapts with the reward head.  Hierarchical approaches---THICK \citep{gumbsch2024thick}, Hieros \citep{mattes2024hieros}, LS-Imagine \citep{li2025lsimagine}---may avoid this by maintaining separate dynamics per temporal scale.  Whether CIVO transfers to transformer-based world models (DreamerV4; \citealt{hafner2025dreamerv4}) depends on whether attention weights co-adapt with temporal embeddings analogously.  Reward-only gradient stopping is an architectural no-op because the reward head contributes zero gradient to $e_{\Delta t}$ (\S\ref{sec:sgs_gradient_analysis}); full-pathway SGS that detaches the dominant observation-decoder gradients constitutes a genuine intervention targeting 93\% of the gradient flow (exp\_018; \S\ref{sec:sgs_implications}).  Whether spectral normalization or other regularization strategies can preserve temporal fidelity while preventing co-adaptation remains an open question.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We have presented a systematic factorial diagnostic of temporal abstraction in DreamerV3, identifying the combination of trained $\dt$-embedding and multi-rate data exposure as the causal factor behind conditioning-induced value overestimation (CIVO). The key findings are:

\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item Every variable-rate imagination configuration degrades relative to the $\dt{=}1$ baseline, with degradation scaling monotonically with $\dt$.
\item A normalized value-to-eval threshold of $\nve \geq 1.5\times$ (35K+ window) achieves 7/8 CIVO classification ($n{=}8$, 87.5\%), with the single false positive arising from a mechanistically distinct non-CIVO collapse. The combination of trained $\dt$-embedding and multi-rate data exposure is the causal factor; unexposed, frozen, or removed embeddings prevent overestimation.
\item The RSSM reward head develops a $2.78\times$ prediction gap between $\dt{=}1$ and $\dt{=}2$ under trained conditioning, providing the systematic bias that drives critic overestimation.
\item The same CIVO trigger produces stochastic downstream outcomes---full collapse, transient CIVO with self-correction, and oscillatory degradation---across seeds of identical configurations.
\item Restricting imagination to $\dt{=}1$ does not fully prevent collapse: one of two seeds fails via a qualitatively distinct non-CIVO pathway (eval-driven with increasing entropy), demonstrating that the trained $\dt$-embedding creates vulnerability through multiple mechanisms.
\end{enumerate}

The contrast with TAWM's success in MPC identifies the feedback architecture as the critical factor: $\dt$-conditioning succeeds when errors are contained by re-planning from real states, but fails when errors compound through imagination-based actor-critic bootstrapping.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
Our experiments focus on a single environment (Walker Walk) and a single world-model architecture (DreamerV3/RSSM). While multi-seed replication establishes robustness within this setting, generalization to other environments and architectures---particularly transformer-based world models such as DreamerV4---remains an open question. The $\nve$ threshold ($1.5\times$, 35K+ window) is sensitive to value overestimation broadly but not perfectly specific to CIVO (7/8, one non-CIVO false positive; margin $0.62$ excluding the outlier); generalization to other tasks and architectures is pending validation.

\paragraph{Future directions.}
Our findings suggest three avenues. First, hierarchical temporal abstraction at the representation level---as in THICK and Hieros---may avoid the flat-conditioning failure mode by maintaining separate dynamics for each temporal scale. Second, gradient decomposition reveals that 93\% of $\dt$-embedding gradients originate from observation decoders, with 0\% from the reward head---making reward-only gradient stopping (exp\_016e) an architectural no-op. Full-pathway gradient stopping targeting observation decoders (exp\_018), which removes the dominant 93\% contribution, is queued to test whether the vulnerability is gradient-level or representation-level. Third, extending this diagnostic framework to transformer-based world models and continuous-control benchmarks would clarify whether CIVO is RSSM-specific or a broader vulnerability of temporal conditioning in imagination-based RL.

\begin{abstract}
Temporal abstraction via variable-rate imagination is a natural extension of world-model reinforcement learning, yet we find that every variable-rate configuration of DreamerV3 degrades performance---despite the success of identical $\Delta t$-conditioning in MPC-based methods. Through a factorial diagnostic on Walker Walk, we identify the combination of trained temporal embedding and multi-rate data exposure as the causal trigger and characterize the resulting failure mode: \emph{conditioning-induced value overestimation} (CIVO). The trained embedding enables the RSSM reward head to develop a $2.78\times$ prediction gap between $\Delta t{=}1$ and $\Delta t{=}2$, which the critic internalizes as optimistic values. Gradient decomposition shows that 93\% of embedding gradients flow through observation decoders (0\% from the reward head), establishing CIVO as representation-driven. A normalized value-to-eval threshold of $1.5\times$ (35K+ training window) achieves 7/8 classification ($n{=}8$, Walker Walk), with the single false positive arising from a mechanistically distinct non-CIVO collapse. The same CIVO trigger produces stochastic downstream outcomes across seeds---full collapse, transient CIVO with self-correction, and oscillatory degradation---from identical configurations. Critically, restricting imagination to $\Delta t{=}1$ does not prevent collapse: one of two seeds fails via a distinct non-CIVO pathway with increasing entropy, revealing that trained temporal conditioning creates vulnerability through multiple mechanisms. Our analysis identifies the feedback architecture---imagination-based actor-critic bootstrapping versus MPC re-planning---as the structural factor that transforms manageable model imprecision into pathological value dynamics.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

TAWM \citep{nhu2025tawm} shows that conditioning a world model on step size $\dt$ yields consistent gains within TD-MPC2's MPC framework \citep{hansen2024tdmpc2}. Can the same $\dt$-conditioning bring temporal abstraction to imagination-based actor-critic methods like DreamerV3 \citep{hafner2023dreamerv3}, where policies are trained entirely in imagined rollouts?

We find that it cannot. Every variable-rate configuration we test degrades performance catastrophically: fixed $\dt{=}2$ collapses to $14\%$ of baseline, fixed $\dt{=}4$ never exceeds $19\%$ of baseline, and a learned Gumbel-Softmax gate collapses to $11\%$. Through a factorial analysis on Walker Walk \citep{tassa2018dmcontrol}, we isolate the combination of a \emph{trained} $\dt$-embedding and multi-rate data exposure as the causal trigger. This trained-and-exposed embedding enables the RSSM reward head to develop a $2.78\times$ prediction gap between $\dt{=}1$ and $\dt{=}2$, which the critic internalizes as optimistic value estimates---a failure mode we term \emph{conditioning-induced value overestimation} (CIVO). A $\nve \geq 1.5\times$ threshold (35K+ window) achieves 7/8 CIVO classification ($n{=}8$, single environment), with one non-CIVO false positive---a failure mode analogous to delusional bias \citep{lu2018nondelusional} but arising from model imprecision amplified by learned temporal conditioning. Gradient decomposition reveals that 93\% of temporal embedding gradients originate from observation decoders rather than the reward head (which contributes 0\%), reframing CIVO as a representation-level phenomenon.

Three further findings complicate the picture: (1)~the same CIVO trigger produces stochastic downstream outcomes---full collapse, transient CIVO with self-correction, and oscillatory degradation---across seeds of identical configurations; (2)~restricting imagination to $\dt{=}1$ while retaining the trained embedding does not prevent collapse, with one of two seeds failing via a qualitatively distinct non-CIVO pathway (evaluation-driven, increasing entropy); and (3)~the contrast with TAWM identifies the feedback architecture---imagination-based bootstrapping versus MPC re-planning---as the structural factor that transforms manageable model imprecision into pathological value dynamics.

