\label{fig:multiple_pathways}
\end{figure}

The mitigation experiment exp\_008e\_v3 restricts imagination to $\dt{=}1$ while retaining the trained $\dt$-embedding for observation conditioning. If CIVO were the sole vulnerability pathway, this restriction should prevent collapse. Results are mixed:

\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Seed 1} (008e\_v3\_s1): Healthy. Eval 869.9 at 105K ($95\%$ of baseline), $\nve = 0.80\times$.
\item \textbf{Seed 2} (008e\_v3\_s2): Collapsed. Eval drops from 715 to 137 at 105K, $\nve = 3.78\times$.
\end{itemize}

The baseline with seed 2 (exp\_009a) is stable at 836.5 at 105K, ruling out inherent seed fragility as the explanation.

\paragraph{Distinct collapse mechanisms.}
The collapse dynamics of 008e\_v3\_s2 differ qualitatively from the classic CIVO observed in 008d (Table~\ref{tab:main_results}, Figure~\ref{fig:multiple_pathways}):

\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{008d (CIVO, Pathway A):} value explosion ($+130\%$) $\rightarrow$ evaluation decline $\rightarrow$ entropy decreases.
\item \textbf{008e\_v3\_s2 (non-CIVO, Pathway B):} evaluation crashes ($-81\%$) $\rightarrow$ value remains stable $\rightarrow$ entropy \emph{increases} ($-5.21 \rightarrow -3.83$).
\end{itemize}

In Pathway A, the critic drives collapse through systematic overestimation; in Pathway B, the actor destabilizes independently while the critic remains initially well-calibrated. Restricting imagination to $\dt{=}1$ prevents the CIVO mechanism---no early $\nve$ spike, no reward amplification---but seed-dependent late-stage policy instability (Pathway B) can still occur.

This case also produces the sole false positive in the $\nve \geq 1.5\times$ classification: 008e\_v3\_s2's late evaluation decline inflates $\nve$ to $3.78\times$ through a non-CIVO mechanism (\S\ref{sec:pathways}).

